Abstract—Head-mounted displays (HMDs) are considered a promising, highly immersive display technology, which has been widely discussed in the context of driving simulation. The literature suggests negative effects of HMD use on simulator sickness and positive effects on perception and presence. However, the reported simulation scenarios with outdated HMD technologies are very different according to today’s standard, which makes it difficult to draw a final conclusion. Therefore, in the present study a comparison between a modern HMD with a screenbased (LED wall) simulator is made in a repeated-measures study with n = 31 subjects. In contrast to previous work, there was no clear evidence for differences between the two simulators in distance and speed perception, simulator sickness, or presence. Most of the evidence in fact suggests equivalence across the two simulators. Accordingly, the present study does not identify any disadvantages of using current HMDs in driving simulation.
Index Terms—driving simulation, virtual reality, headmounted display, simulator sickness, presence, perception
I. INTRODUCTION
In driving simulation the goal is usually to provide the driver with a realistic impression of the displayed scene. Human perception and information processing are central in this context. A driving simulator is required to correctly reproduce sensory inputs received by the driver [1]. [2] goes so far as to say that evaluating driving behavior is only sensible if the sensations received in the driving simulator correspond with those which would be received in reality.
Visual information is key to the driving task [3]; distance [4] and speed perception [5] are especially important as they are required for tasks such as stopping, overtaking, or maintaining speed. Both are based on visual input information [6]. Regarding distance perception, pictorial cues and binocular cues can be distinguished. While pictorial distance cues, such as the relative size of objects or occlusion, are comparatively

easy to implement, binocular cues are more difficult to reproduce. Convergence is a binocular depth cue which refers to the angle at which the eyes are relative to each other when focusing [7]. However, this is thought to be relevant only at very short distances of 2-3 m. Accommodation refers to the contraction of the ciliary muscle. In a moving environment, also motion-dependent cues influence depth perception, such as motion parallax: Objects which are closer to the observer seem to move faster than objects in a far distance [7].
When it comes to speed perception, optic flow is believed to be the main determining variable. It is generated by the motion of an observer and his surroundings. When driving and looking in the direction of travel, environment objects seem to move out of the central field of view (FOV) into periphery [7]. The faster objects move along the optic flow, the faster the intrinsic movement is perceived. Summarizing the information presented above, visual perception undoubtedly determines driving behavior. Driving simulators should replicate realworld driving behavior as good as possible (also referred to as behavioral validity [8]), as only then it is possible to draw inferences on the real-world context.
Visual perception is largely determined by simulator fidelity, commonly understood as the capability of the simulator to reproduce the real-world driving context [8]. Display hardware can contribute to the correct representation of real-world cues or its deterioration [6]. Both software and hardware elements determine visual fidelity. As the present study is aimed at investigating influences of the display type, we will focus on effects of hardware technology.
A. Display technologies in driving simulators
Among the most frequently used display solutions in driving simulators are screens (mostly LCD panels), projectors, or

head-mounted displays (HMDs). HMDs offer a cheap solution as all elements of a real vehicle can be displayed virtually, hence no physical mockup is required. [9] proposed that 40 % of the studies investigated could be conducted using HMDs. They mention as a benefit that HMDs are, at least partially, able to provide binocular depth cues. Perception may thus be enhanced with HMDs. McIntire et al. concluded in their 2014 review [10] that 60 % of the studies show beneficial effects of binocular displays. However, [11] proclaim that the mere fact that a three-dimensional world is transferred to a two-dimensional sort of display always leaves room to ambiguity in sensory cues. Even state-of-the-art stereoscopic displays are incapable of delivering an appropriately variable focus, and thus, provide vergence cues without an adequate accommodation, causing a vergence-accommodation conflict.
[9] also state that the sense of presence should be increased with HMDs, which was also reported by [12]. The sense of presence is defined as the sense of being there in a virtual environment [13]. It is assumed to depend on immersion, which in turn is understood as the degree to which the realworld is shut out by a setup [14]. The higher the sense of presence, the more realistic drivers are expected to behave within the virtual environment [12]. The literature on effects of using HMDs on presence is ambiguous, however, as effects could not be confirmed by all studies [15], [16].
The incidence of simulator sickness is often reported to be increased with HMDs [12], [16]–[18], forming a potential disadvantage. Simulator sickness is a physical discomfort that can occur when the cues received in a virtual environment do not match the expectations of the receiver [19]. Sickness is to be reduced to minimize dropouts and to preserve validity [20], [21]. Also regarding effects on simulator sickness, effects of HMDs have not been fully elucidated. [15] and [22] could not confirm any effects of using HMDs on simulator sickness. [23] even found increased simulator sickness in projection screens when compared to HMDs.
Besides displays effects, there are some practical implications when using HMDs. First, when wearing HMDs, participants are always exposed to extra weight and often extra cables, which can lead to an unnatural feeling and less head movements. Second, the FOV is theoretically always limited with HMDs (approx. 110° with most to-date HMD solutions, approx. 200° in the real world [24]), potentially reducing optic flow, while peripheral information is available with other displays. We also found that many research papers on HMDs are comparatively old or used low-fidelity HMDs, which is often associated with lower image resolution [9], contrast, FOV, etc. Thus, the differences found between HMDs and other displays may also be attributed to different hardware properties. The horizontal FOV, for instance, was reported to influence presence [25], simulator sickness [23], [26], [27], distance and speed perception [6], [28], [29]. Image resolution can influence distance perception by atmospheric depth [30]. Optic flow should depend on the horizontal FOV [7].
Another limitation of previous studies is that there were differences in the display technology compared (projection:

[15], [16], [23]; displays: [12], [31]). When multiple screens are combined, display boundaries are usually visible, which reduces the immersion of the visualization. Projection systems, on the other hand, are often low contrast, which can also have a negative impact on immersion. In the present study, we therefore compare the HMD to a highly immersive and novel visualization technology in driving simulation: the LED wall.
LED walls have been previously used especially in advertisement but are today also used in television studios. Smearing effects can be reduced at low latency and realistic blending can be realized, which is a common issue with projectors. Furthermore, due to the high image contrast of the LED technology, it is possible to reproduce colors and lighting conditions realistically.
B. Research gap
In summary, HMDs offer a cheap and easy-to-implement solution for visualizing driving scenes and thus hold great potentials for the area of driving simulations. Findings on effects of using HMDs, however, are so far ambiguous and require clarification. Positive and negative effects of HMDs have been proclaimed but were often not replicated. However, studies that did not find effects of HMDs have not provided statistical evidence for the equivalence of different visualization systems either. Using Bayesian hypothesis testing instead of frequentist statistics (see section ”Statistical analysis”), we aim to also address statistical equivalence in the present study to identify whether there really is no disadvantage in using HMDs.
Many studies have been conducted with outdated HMDs, which were also disadvantageous in terms of hardware aspects beyond the display type. For the reasons outlined above, we conducted a new comparative study of a state-of-the-art HMD with an LED wall. As described above, the LED wall technology is rather new in the context of driving simulation and has therefore not been included in previous investigations on effects of the display type.
C. The present study
A within-subject study was conducted in which subjects experienced an LED wall simulator and an HMD simulator in succession in different driving scenarios. We applied the following hypotheses to the study: H1: Distance perception is more accurate in the HMD simulator due to the availability of binocular depth cues. H2: Speed perception is more accurate in the LED wall simulator due to a larger FOV and peripheral vision. H3: The sense of presence is higher in the HMD simulator. H4: Incidence and severity of simulator sickness are higher in the HMD simulator.
II. METHODOLOGY
A. Sample
Out of the initial sample, 22 participants dropped out, 6 of them for technical reasons. Another 16 participants dropped out due to simulator sickness; 9 while using the HMD

simulator, and another 7 while using the LED wall simulator. 31 participants completed the study, of which 3 were female and 28 were male. Participants were aged between 20 and 57 years, with an average age of 31 years. B. Materials
The software Unreal [32] was used for visualization. Object and agent information and trajectories were provided by proprietary software modules to the graphics engine. Hardware components as well as software integration of the steering wheel and pedals were largely standardized among the mockups. The frame rate was limited to constant 60 Hz in both simulators to avoid fluctuation and the achieved contrast of the HMD using OLED technology is comparable to that of the LED wall (approx. 1,000,000:1). A limitation was that the brake in the VR simulator worked using a force sensor while the brake in the LED wall simulator worked using a stroke sensor. The brakes were manually calibrated to ensure the same braking response and effect in both simulators.
The HMD driving simulator (Figure 1) consisted of a seat box built from a BMW 5-series vehicle mockup. A matching G30 design model was displayed in the virtual scene. For visualization, the Varjo VR-3 was used [33]. According to Varjo’s own statement [33], the VR-3 currently offers the highest resolution as well as one of the widest FOVs (1920×1920 px per eye and 115°) on the market. The VR3 should therefore deliver the fairest possible comparison. Including the head-band, the VR-3 has a total weight of 944 g. The visual representation of limbs was not realized in the given setup, therefore participants were unable to see their own hands. The LED wall simulator (Figure 1) was equipped with a full-vehicle BMW 5-series mockup. The simulator was equipped with a small motion system, which was disabled to allow comparability with the static HMD setup. An LED wall with a horizontal FOV of 310 was used, with an overall resolution of approximately 26 pixels per degree. Rear vision was implemented with LCD display mirrors.
