IV. DISCUSSION
In the present study, an HMD and an LED wall simulator were compared with regard to possible differences in perception, simulator sickness, and presence. Contrary to our expectations, we did not observe any positive effects of HMD use on distance perception and no negative effects on speed perception. There was moderate evidence for equivalence across the two simulators in the blind following and distance keeping tasks and in the acceleration task. In the passive speed perception and the egocentric distance perception tasks, we found anecdotal evidence for equivalence. In the target braking task, there was anecdotal evidence for a better perception in the LED wall simulator. The distance estimation tasks on the highway and the egocentric distance perception tasks were mainly concerned with longer distances, where binocular cues tend not to play a role. For this reason, the stereoscopic presentation in the HMD hardly provides an advantage here, which is why the absence of an effect is not too surprising, even though positive effects might have been expected due to the lower distance to the display medium in the HMD simulator. The better performance in the LED wall simulator in the braking task

is rather surprising considering that small distances (< 10 m) matter here (therefore also binocular cues matter) and the display is closer in the HMD simulator, reducing the conflict of focusing distance (display) and presented distance. However, the evidence was only anecdotal.
Regarding speed perception, positive effects of the LED wall simulator were expected due to the wider FOV, which could not be confirmed. [6] suggested that a 120° horizontal FOV would be required to correctly perceive speeds. The HMD simulator offered a FOV (115°) that was already very close to this recommendation, so the difference may simply not have been large enough to make an impact.
There was evidence for equivalence across the two simulators with regard to the incidence and severity of simulator sickness, which did not match our initial expectations. However, other authors could neither identify any negative effects of HMD usage [15], [22], [23]. Furthermore, the present study, as outlined throughout the introduction, used a stateof-the-art high-fidelity HMD, which was not the case in most previous investigations. Effects found in earlier studies could also be attributable to other properties of the used HMDs, such as low image resolution. There was also a scenario effect: simulator sickness in the urban scenario was significantly higher compared to the other scenarios, replicating [21].
